WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:16.478
next day, the Widow Douglas washed the mud off my clothes.

2
00:00:17.478 --> 00:00:21.962
She didn't scold me, but she looked so sad that I decided to try to be good for

3
00:00:22.002 --> 00:00:22.342
a while.

4
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:25.284
I called my father "Pap."

5
00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:27.886
No one had seen him for over a year.

6
00:00:28.627 --> 00:00:29.688
That was fine with me.

7
00:00:31.176 --> 00:00:33.957
I didn't want to see Pap again because he beat me.

8
00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:36.358
Then some people in St.

9
00:00:36.418 --> 00:00:39.299
Petersburg told me that they'd found his body in the river.

10
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.862
They said the drowned man was just his size, with ragged clothes and long hair like Pap's.

11
00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:51.745
But they really couldn't recognize the face because the body had been in the water for so long.

12
00:00:53.846 --> 00:00:56.066
They buried the drowned man on the riverbank.

13
00:00:57.027 --> 00:00:59.548
A few days later, though, I thought of something.

14
00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:04.006
The townspeople told me they found the body floating on his back.

15
00:01:04.806 --> 00:01:10.329
But I had once been told that drowned men floated on their faces and only drowned women floated

16
00:01:10.389 --> 00:01:10.950
on their backs.

17
00:01:12.451 --> 00:01:16.553
So I decided the body must have been a woman, dressed up in men's clothes.

18
00:01:17.774 --> 00:01:20.875
After that, I was worried that my father would come back someday.

19
00:01:22.636 --> 00:01:27.419
I still played robbers sometimes with Tom and the other boys, but I was getting bored with it.

20
00:01:28.279 --> 00:01:29.180
It was all pretending.

21
00:01:31.388 --> 00:01:36.371
On Saturday Tom told us that we were going to ambush some rich merchants who had two hundred

22
00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:41.695
elephants, six hundred camels, and a thousand mules loaded with diamonds.

23
00:01:42.576 --> 00:01:44.377
But that was just a story he made up.

24
00:01:45.257 --> 00:01:48.219
Really we were just ambushing a Sunday school picnic.

25
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.922
We chased the children away, but only got their doughnuts and jam.

26
00:01:52.942 --> 00:01:57.846
"I don't see any diamonds," I said to Tom.

27
00:01:58.846 --> 00:02:02.149
"Why, they're all right in front of you," Tom answered.

28
00:02:02.169 --> 00:02:04.810
"And the elephants and camels too.

29
00:02:06.031 --> 00:02:10.554
Some magicians must have turned them into a Sunday school picnic before we got here."

30
00:02:11.615 --> 00:02:14.797
Then Tom told me how magicians use genies to help them.

31
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:18.699
"Let's get some genies to come and help us," I said.

32
00:02:20.461 --> 00:02:24.483
Tom explained that you have to rub a magic lamp to make a genie appear.

33
00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.565
Then the genie will do whatever you want.

34
00:02:29.286 --> 00:02:31.428
A few days later I thought I would try it.

35
00:02:32.304 --> 00:02:36.747
I went out into the woods with an old lamp and I rubbed it and rubbed it, but

36
00:02:36.947 --> 00:02:37.607
nothing happened.

37
00:02:39.608 --> 00:02:42.530
I think Tom really believed these things, but I didn't.

38
00:02:48.093 --> 00:02:50.054
Several months passed, and winter came.

39
00:02:50.874 --> 00:02:51.635
I went to school.

40
00:02:52.415 --> 00:02:56.598
At first I hated it, but soon I could spell and read and write a little.

41
00:02:57.558 --> 00:03:00.820
I could say the multiplication tables up to six times seven.

42
00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:03.746
Then, school became easier for me.

43
00:03:05.187 --> 00:03:07.768
I was also getting used to the Widow Douglas' house.

44
00:03:08.749 --> 00:03:10.811
The widow told me that I was doing well.

45
00:03:11.851 --> 00:03:13.513
She said she wasn't ashamed of me.

46
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:18.436
One morning at breakfast, I accidentally knocked over the salt shaker.

47
00:03:19.397 --> 00:03:21.158
It is very bad luck to spill salt.

48
00:03:22.919 --> 00:03:28.183
So I reached for some of the salt to throw over my left shoulder because that's supposed to

49
00:03:28.243 --> 00:03:29.364
keep the bad luck away.

50
00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.681
But Miss Watson stopped me.

51
00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:35.502
"Take your hands away, Huckleberry," she scolded.

52
00:03:35.522 --> 00:03:38.223
"What a mess you are always making!"

53
00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:43.605
As I left the house after breakfast, I wondered when my bad luck would arrive.

54
00:03:44.646 --> 00:03:46.687
Then I saw footprints in the new snow.

55
00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:53.089
When I bent down to take a closer look, I noticed a cross made with big nails on

56
00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:54.290
the heel of the left boot.

57
00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:55.930
I knew that boot!

58
00:03:56.871 --> 00:03:59.612
I ran as fast as I could to Judge Thatcher's house.

59
00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:04.992
"Why, my boy, you're all out of breath," he said.

60
00:04:05.012 --> 00:04:07.594
"Did you come to get some of your money?"

61
00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:09.916
"No sir," I said.

62
00:04:09.936 --> 00:04:11.457
"I don't want it!

63
00:04:12.338 --> 00:04:14.120
I want to give all the money to you."

64
00:04:15.561 --> 00:04:17.282
Judge Thatcher was very surprised.

65
00:04:18.343 --> 00:04:20.125
He asked me whether anything was wrong.

66
00:04:21.746 --> 00:04:27.331
"Please take it," I begged him, "and don't ask me anything— then I won't have to tell you

67
00:04:27.391 --> 00:04:28.012
any lies."

68
00:04:30.672 --> 00:04:34.615
Finally the judge agreed to buy my money from me for one dollar.

69
00:04:35.435 --> 00:04:38.217
He put the dollar in my hand and I left his house.

70
00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:44.382
Miss Watson's black slave Jim, had a magic hairball as big as your fist.

71
00:04:45.282 --> 00:04:47.564
It had come from the fourth stomach of an ox.

72
00:04:48.624 --> 00:04:51.987
Jim said there was a spirit inside it that could see the future.

73
00:04:53.108 --> 00:04:54.609
I went to see Jim that evening.

74
00:04:55.549 --> 00:04:59.192
I told him that I knew Pap was back and I wanted to know what he would do.

75
00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.382
Jim got out his hairball and said some strange words to it that I couldn't understand.

76
00:05:06.343 --> 00:05:08.503
Then he held it out and dropped it on the floor.

77
00:05:09.824 --> 00:05:14.026
He got down on his knees, put his ear against the hairball, and listened closely.

78
00:05:14.946 --> 00:05:15.606
He shook his head.

79
00:05:15.626 --> 00:05:19.968
"The hairball won't talk without money," Jim said.

80
00:05:21.429 --> 00:05:24.450
I gave Jim an old brass quarter that I had in my pocket.

81
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.752
It wasn't a real coin, but maybe the hairball would take it.

82
00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.861
Jim put the quarter under the hairball and listened again.

83
00:05:33.781 --> 00:05:37.122
This time, Jim said the hairball would tell me my whole fortune.

84
00:05:38.983 --> 00:05:45.385
The hairball talked to Jim, and Jim repeated it to me: "Your father doesn't know yet what he's

85
00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:48.646
going to do, but you're going to be all right.

86
00:05:49.586 --> 00:05:53.207
You'll have a lot of trouble in your life, but also joy.

87
00:05:54.127 --> 00:05:56.808
And keep away from water as much as you can."

88
00:05:57.576 --> 00:06:02.523
But when I lit my candle and went up to my room that night, there was
